title: 'Consequence: a fork made in such a view vanishes from it.'
pid: a4ad022b-258c-48c3-879d-f9c05ef61cb1
body: |2-
  Editing a foreign child under a foreign root forks it, and the
  rerender, following the author, shows the original again, unedited;
  only its stats (one subscriber, one overrider) hint at the clone. A
  second edit there is refused (ForkAlreadyExists, naming the clone).
  Editing the root itself is different: the save makes the clone the
  view's root, and that tree then follows one's own overrides. This is
  what Jeff's parenthetical asks for, but it will surprise someone.
  Switched to the user's overrides, the same edit shows the clone, with
  the edit, in the original's place, which is what the switch is for.
  Since 2026-11-14 the save says so: a fork made below a root, in a
  tree following another owner's overrides, earns a warning naming the
  clone's skgrepo, the owner followed, and the switch
  ('forks_the_view_will_not_show' in 'server/from_text/fork.rs'). This
  is what a third party meets on replying in another's unread list, as
  Ed did in Jeff's: the original drawn, nothing filed, and now the
  warning. Showing the third party's fork there instead would break
  the decision above, so I built only the warning.
